1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:03,491
Why is the area of a
circle pi r squared?

2
00:00:04,537 --> 00:00:06,807
Cut the circle into thin rings,

3
00:00:07,454 --> 00:00:08,256
like an onion.

4
00:00:09,165 --> 00:00:11,493
Each ring is about
as long as its

5
00:00:11,525 --> 00:00:14,044
circumference, and very thin.

6
00:00:15,166 --> 00:00:17,416
Straighten each
ring into a strip,

7
00:00:18,106 --> 00:00:18,936
and stack them:

8
00:00:19,826 --> 00:00:23,001
shortest on top,
longest at the bottom.

9
00:00:24,037 --> 00:00:25,689
They pile up into something

10
00:00:25,759 --> 00:00:27,329
close to a triangle.

11
00:00:29,106 --> 00:00:30,586
Now make the rings thinner.

12
00:00:31,306 --> 00:00:32,333
Smaller steps.

13
00:00:33,349 --> 00:00:34,819
As the rings get thinner,

14
00:00:35,459 --> 00:00:36,579
the steps vanish,

15
00:00:37,449 --> 00:00:39,487
and the stack
becomes a triangle.

16
00:00:40,888 --> 00:00:43,517
Its height is the radius, r.

17
00:00:44,503 --> 00:00:46,513
Its base is the outermost ring,

18
00:00:47,013 --> 00:00:50,982
unrolled: the
circumference, two pi r.

19
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:54,526
A triangle's area is half

20
00:00:54,573 --> 00:00:56,185
the base times the height.

21
00:00:57,211 --> 00:00:58,611
Half of two pi r,

22
00:00:58,731 --> 00:01:01,580
times r, is pi r squared.

23
00:01:02,895 --> 00:01:06,517
Same area as the
circle: pi r squared.
